# GEAR stellar feedback examples

Test cases for the GEAR stellar feedback mechanisms, organised by
mechanism directory. Each subdirectory is self-contained and carries its
own README with the configure line, the run command and how to check the
result; start there once you have picked one below. `radiation_table_reader.py`
in this directory is shared by several of the analytic-check scripts below;
it is not an example on its own.

## Supernovae

No shipped example yet.

## `StellarWinds/`

| Example | Tests |
|---|---|
| `StellarWindBubble` | Bubble propagation driven by stellar-wind injection in a homogeneous, periodic gas box. |
| `StellarWindInjection` | Wind energy injection in isolation, on a cartesian IC grid, with no external influence. |

## `RadiationPressure/`

| Example | Tests |
|---|---|
| `RadiationPressureShellExpansion` | The radiation-pressure momentum channel alone (photoionization, cooling and gravity off). Compared against the momentum-conserving analytic solution of Krumholz & Matzner (2009, ApJ 703, 1352). |

## `HIIRegions/`

| Example | Tests |
|---|---|
| `StromgrenSphere` | Equilibrium Stromgren radius and Spitzer (1978) D-type expansion for a single source in a uniform box. The simplest case, and the base configuration the others vary. |
| `Starbench` | The STARBENCH late-phase D-type expansion test (Bisbas et al. 2015, MNRAS 453, 1324), checked against that paper's own semi-empirical solution rather than Spitzer, which over-predicts at late times. |
| `Hu2017` | Four co-located sources in a dense uniform medium (Hu et al. 2017, MNRAS 471, 2151, Appendix A; the same test appears in Smith et al. 2021). Also the setup used for cadence studies. |
| `ClumpSmith2021` | Erosion of a dense clump by a nearby source (Smith et al. 2021, MNRAS 506, 3882, Fig. 2). Ships matrix-analysis scripts as well as a single-run one. |
| `StromgrenSphereCosmo` | The `StromgrenSphere` box with cosmology enabled: an identity tier at a ~ 1 that must reproduce the non-cosmological result, and a high-redshift tier checked against the analytic formulas at the instantaneous physical density. |
| `TaskGraphPair` | A source whose region spans a pair of top-level cells, exercising the neighbour search across a cell boundary. No analytic reference; correct means reaching `time_end` with no error. |
| `TaskGraphCluster` | Many co-located sources competing for gas in the same region, exercising the locking in the star loops. No analytic reference; correct means reaching `time_end` with no error. |

`Starbench`, `Hu2017`, and `ClumpSmith2021` need `--with-cooling=grackle_1`:
at mode 1 and above the photon budget is priced against the neutral
hydrogen fraction from the species network rather than against total
hydrogen, which is part of the physics they compare. The others run with
any mode; each README states which it needs.

## `ISRF/`

Interstellar radiation field (photoelectric heating and its FUV/LW
propagation and dissipation), independent of the HII photoionization
mechanism above.

| Example | Tests |
|---|---|
| `ISRFPhotoelectricHeating` | Photoelectric heating in isolation (injection, no propagation), against real Grackle cooling. |
| `ISRFInjectionConservation` | Injection alone (propagation off), checking the field is conserved. |
| `ISRFHyperbolicPropagation` | The hyperbolic (Cattaneo-type flux-relaxation, M1 closure) propagation scheme against its own discrete steady-state prediction. |
| `ISRFCausalReach` | Propagation with the field initialised to zero, checking the causal reach of the hyperbolic scheme. |
| `ISRFPropagationSpeedSweep` | A `c_hyp` sweep of the propagation scheme, from `c_hyp ~ c` down to `c_hyp << c`. |
| `ISRFOpticallyThin` | Propagation in the optically-thin corner of parameter space. |
| `ISRFDissipation` | The negativity-triggered dissipation mechanism. |
| `ISRFDissipationExtreme` | Dissipation under an extreme parameter combination. |
| `ISRFMultiBinDissipation` | Two hydrostatic gas phases at different SWIFT timestep bins, with sources placed to probe cross-bin dissipation behaviour. |
| `ISRFShearAsymmetry` | A stabilised shear layer carrying two seeded Gaussian FUV/LW blobs, checking the propagation and dissipation scheme stays isotropic. |
| `ISRFSourceTurnOff` | Field decay once the source star dies, same setup as `ISRFHyperbolicPropagation` otherwise. |
| `ISRFH2Photodissociation` | H2 photodissociation by a star's propagated LW field in cold, zero-metallicity gas, against the shielded rate (Draine & Bertoldi 1996, Eq. 37, with Grackle's Wolcott-Green & Haiman 2019 exponent). Needs `--with-cooling=grackle_2` or above. |

Reconfiguring rebuilds `swift` in place, so check before you start a run
that depends on the current binary.
